1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,097
The plan everyone in the room agreed with

2
00:00:03,097 --> 00:00:05,806
is the one you should attack first.

3
00:00:05,806 --> 00:00:08,903
Here's how the best teams red-team their own

4
00:00:08,903 --> 00:00:12,000
strategy — before reality does it for them.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,400
In the next few minutes you'll get three

6
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:20,800
red-team moves your team can run on any

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,200
big decision, why they work, and how they

8
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:29,050
turn into a capability your organization owns.

9
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:33,450
The third one is the one most leaders

10
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:34,000
skip.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,333
Most strategies don't fail on bad logic.

12
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:43,286
They fail on one assumption nobody was paid

13
00:00:43,286 --> 00:00:48,238
to attack — and on a room that

14
00:00:48,238 --> 00:00:50,095
agreed too fast.

15
00:00:50,095 --> 00:00:53,190
That fast agreement isn't safety.

16
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:58,143
It's groupthink, and it has a name because

17
00:00:58,143 --> 00:01:00,000
it's that common.

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,608
Move one: the premortem.

19
00:01:03,608 --> 00:01:10,824
Don't ask what could go wrong — that

20
00:01:10,824 --> 00:01:12,627
invites optimism.

21
00:01:12,627 --> 00:01:17,137
State the failure as fact.

22
00:01:17,137 --> 00:01:24,353
It's eighteen months from now and this decision

23
00:01:24,353 --> 00:01:26,157
failed completely.

24
00:01:26,157 --> 00:01:30,667
Now every person writes why.

25
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:37,882
The cheapest place to find a fatal flaw

26
00:01:37,882 --> 00:01:45,098
is here, on the whiteboard, not after the

27
00:01:45,098 --> 00:01:46,000
launch.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,617
Move two: the key assumptions check.

29
00:01:51,617 --> 00:01:59,106
Rewrite the plan as a list of beliefs,

30
00:01:59,106 --> 00:02:03,787
each a single falsifiable sentence.

31
00:02:03,787 --> 00:02:11,277
Score each one: how confident are we, and

32
00:02:11,277 --> 00:02:17,830
how bad is it if we're wrong?

33
00:02:17,830 --> 00:02:25,319
The low-confidence, high-consequence assumptions are your real strategy

34
00:02:25,319 --> 00:02:26,255
risk.

35
00:02:26,255 --> 00:02:30,000
Everything else is noise.

36
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Move three, the one leaders skip: assign the

37
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:37,875
dissent.

38
00:02:37,875 --> 00:02:44,875
Don't hope someone speaks up — give one

39
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:51,875
person the job of arguing the other side.

40
00:02:51,875 --> 00:02:58,875
The devil's advocate isn't new; institutions formalized it

41
00:02:58,875 --> 00:03:05,875
centuries ago precisely because volunteered dissent never comes.

42
00:03:05,875 --> 00:03:12,000
Make it a role, not a personality.

43
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,778
Run these and something changes.

44
00:03:15,778 --> 00:03:21,822
The team stops defending decisions and starts pressure-testing

45
00:03:21,822 --> 00:03:22,578
them.

46
00:03:22,578 --> 00:03:28,622
For a business, that's the real opportunity: decision

47
00:03:28,622 --> 00:03:34,667
quality becomes a repeatable capability you own, not

48
00:03:34,667 --> 00:03:37,689
a report you rent.

49
00:03:37,689 --> 00:03:43,733
Wins compound, and the cost of being wrong

50
00:03:43,733 --> 00:03:46,000
drops every quarter.

51
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,400
Here's the one judgment call a video can't

52
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,800
make for you: which decision, right now, is

53
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:57,550
too important to leave unchallenged?

54
00:03:57,550 --> 00:04:00,850
Bring that one to a session.

55
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:05,250
Comment the decision you're facing, and grab the

56
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:08,000
premortem checklist in the description.
